WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.230
- Well, good morning folks.

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:03.670
You're coming with me for
another day in my life

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:04.503
in the hospital.

00:00:04.503 --> 00:00:06.150
I get to show you some cool aspects

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:09.460
of how we've adapted care
in the era of COVID-19.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:11.203
I'm gonna shower, I promise.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:13.673
Thank you.

00:00:13.673 --> 00:00:15.610
I'm going to give a huge thank
you to Clorox for sponsoring

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:16.730
this part of the video.

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:18.976
Before heading out to
work and starting my day

00:00:18.976 --> 00:00:21.020
and leaving Bear unattended,

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.790
I want to make sure I leave
my house really clean.

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:25.270
So I'm gonna show you how
I clean my commonly touched

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:29.640
surfaces like this countertop
right here using Clorox bleach

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:30.640
with Cloro Max.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:33.700
I actually premixed the solution
as directed right there,

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.280
have my gloves to keep me safe.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:38.964
I dip my white rag and I
wipe it down. Wipe it down.

00:00:38.964 --> 00:00:40.337
Now, in all seriousness,

00:00:40.337 --> 00:00:42.510
you're gonna get proven disinfection.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:45.200
You're gonna kill 99.9% of germs

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:46.450
with only a third of a cup.

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:48.350
Just make sure that you follow
directions really carefully

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:49.183
on that one.

00:00:49.183 --> 00:00:51.530
You could use this on a
lot of high touch areas.

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:53.320
Remember I told you those are the areas,

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.160
Bear, you're distracting me, buddy.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.070
Those are the areas that are gonna have

00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:57.970
the most germs on them.

00:00:57.970 --> 00:00:59.840
So you think faucets, doorknobs,

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:02.102
just a lot of non porous
surfaces that get touched

00:01:02.102 --> 00:01:03.907
quite frequently, and as they say,

00:01:03.907 --> 00:01:06.200
"When it really matters, trust Clorox."

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:08.250
Let's give Clorox a huge
thank you for sponsoring

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:09.520
this portion of the video.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:10.910
I'm about to hop in the shower,

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:14.530
clean myself, and get ready
to take on another day

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:16.016
at the hospital.

00:01:16.016 --> 00:01:18.480
There was a very crazy
start to the morning,

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.770
I was woken up several
times throughout the night

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:23.270
by news agencies reaching out,

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:26.020
asking if I'm able to do
television this morning,

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:27.401
I'm actually going into the office.

00:01:27.401 --> 00:01:29.730
So I won't be able to do media.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:32.498
Today is an outpatient
family medicine day.

00:01:32.498 --> 00:01:33.331
TGIF.

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:37.080
Donna, are you ready?
- I am ready!

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:37.963
- What are we doing?

00:01:37.963 --> 00:01:38.980
- We're giving you your flu shot.

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.360
- Why?
- Keep you healthy.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:41.968
- Are you gonna be really
rough with the needle?

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:43.118
- I'm gonna be so gentle.

00:01:43.118 --> 00:01:44.460
I think I might jab you a little.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:47.600
- She's gonna jab me, but that's
because I misbehave a lot.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:48.780
This is the flu shot.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.020
We're gonna close the door
cause it's getting serious.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:53.289
Be rough with me, let's go,
pull it up as hard as you need.

00:01:53.289 --> 00:01:54.410
It's stretchy.

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:55.427
- I want you to relax.

00:01:55.427 --> 00:01:56.640
- I'm relaxed.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.673
- One, two, three. Huge.

00:01:59.568 --> 00:02:01.260
(beep)

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:02.540
Perfect.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:03.373
- That was really good. That was smooth.

00:02:03.373 --> 00:02:04.649
- You did not even move.
- That was smooth.

00:02:04.649 --> 00:02:06.907
While we're sitting
here, I have my bone pen.

00:02:06.907 --> 00:02:07.740
(laughing)

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.810
But in all seriousness, everyone at home,

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:11.400
please get your flu shot.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:12.940
We really need it now more than ever.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:14.260
It's COVID-19 season.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:15.950
And while this flu shot
does not prevent you

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:17.130
from getting COVID-19,

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:19.390
it does prevent you from getting
a virus which may look like

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:21.610
COVID-19, which will lead
to a ton of confusion.

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:24.200
On top of that, with hospitals being busy,

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:25.780
handling COVID-19 patients,

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:28.230
we don't want you to get sick
with the flu and then have to

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:29.530
come into the hospital for care.

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:32.450
It's funny, I'm actually
in an exam room right now,

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:35.030
but as you can tell,
there's no patient here.

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:38.250
But there is a computer and
this computer has a webcam

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:41.410
and a microphone all set up
for virtual patient visits,

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:43.070
which we've been doing a lot of.

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:45.440
In fact, telemedicine
has absolutely blown up

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:47.800
during the COVID-19
pandemic and rightfully so.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:50.660
A lot of things you can take
care of by a virtual visit.

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:52.670
Having a conversation about medications,

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:54.550
perhaps looking at some rashes,

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:56.260
there's all sorts of
guidance that we can give

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:57.380
through a virtual visit.

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:59.866
That being said, a virtual
visit does not replace

00:02:59.866 --> 00:03:01.844
a visit face to face with your doctor.

00:03:01.844 --> 00:03:05.090
There's all sorts of benefits
to seeing a doctor in person,

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:07.240
including getting an
accurate physical exam,

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:08.980
reading body cues, having a better

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:10.380
doctor-patient connection.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.460
The physical exam is probably
the most important though.

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:13.508
- Yo, Tia?

00:03:13.508 --> 00:03:14.419
- Yeah?

00:03:14.419 --> 00:03:15.690
- You know what time it is?

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:17.620
- What time is it? (laughs)

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:19.170
- It's lunchtime. I'm treating today.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:21.670
- We're chilling with our
favorite doctor, Mike, here.

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:22.970
- What do you wanna eat?

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:24.560
You always tell me I
don't order enough food.

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:26.804
- Where's the buffalo
pizza you normally order?

00:03:26.804 --> 00:03:30.280
(dramatic music)

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:32.500
- Team! What are we eating?

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:34.610
- That sandwiches place
down on Main Street.

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:35.790
- Okay, sandwiches?

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:36.623
- Yeah.

00:03:36.623 --> 00:03:37.610
- Okay, Greg, any votes?

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:39.543
- Not really.

00:03:39.543 --> 00:03:42.599
(dramatic music)

00:03:42.599 --> 00:03:44.801
- Tell me how COVID
has affected your life.

00:03:44.801 --> 00:03:48.946
- It has affected me,
I would say personally.

00:03:48.946 --> 00:03:49.779
- Okay.

00:03:49.779 --> 00:03:51.700
- And emotionally.

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:55.340
At the end of it, if we work
really hard to stay safe

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:57.440
and follow guidelines,

00:03:57.440 --> 00:03:59.822
we'll be better off in the long run.

00:03:59.822 --> 00:04:01.840
And that's something that gets me by.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.390
I wanna show you guys real quick,

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:05.780
some COVID related changes in the office.

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:07.030
This is our waiting area.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:09.890
Social distancing is important.

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:12.160
We're also not having patients wait here.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:14.120
They actually call us
from the parking lot.

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:15.160
Let us know that they're here.

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:17.470
We get them checked in,
meet them right by the door.

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:20.020
Do a temperature check,
ask a few questions,

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:22.530
bring them in following a one way path.

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:25.320
Patients can only walk
through this hallway,

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:28.610
exit through there, enter through there.

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:30.300
This is cool. I like pointing away.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:31.700
Stacy, why are you laughing?

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:34.660
Just stop laughing at me for one second.

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:37.180
Vivian, how do you feel about
5 million people seeing you?

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:38.860
- It's a very trying experience.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:39.693
(laughing)

00:04:39.693 --> 00:04:42.200
- So I just had a really
interesting patient encounter

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:45.357
with a young gentleman who was
complaining of easy bruising

00:04:45.357 --> 00:04:47.910
and actually bruising that
happened over the last weekend

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:50.480
without any trauma or
any inciting incident.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:52.720
And in these conditions, a
lot of people start going

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:54.951
to clotting issues, platelet dysfunction,

00:04:54.951 --> 00:04:56.740
even cancer, sometimes.

00:04:56.740 --> 00:04:57.900
What's interesting with this patient,

00:04:57.900 --> 00:04:59.520
all those things have
already been ruled out

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:01.180
because they were seen by hematologist,

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:02.290
who is a blood specialist.

00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:04.430
This is the second time they're
being evaluated for this

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:05.700
approximately a year later.

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:07.530
And right away, my mind
jumped to something

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:09.960
that was actually learned
in medical school.

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:11.880
And that's Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome,

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.580
which is a connective
tissue disorder that causes

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:18.180
hypermobile joints, skin
issues like easy bruising.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:20.850
So I asked the patient, if
he has any hypermobility,

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:23.110
and he said, "Oh yeah, I
consider myself double jointed."

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:26.070
He's able to bring his thumb
all the way to his wrist.

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:28.210
The skin is very elastic, very loose.

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:32.300
So now we're beginning the
evaluation to see if he has EDS.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:34.270
But unless we think outside of the box,

00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:37.150
when a patient comes in, you
can miss a diagnosis like EDS.

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:38.480
That's why it's important
to get a thorough,

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:40.410
thorough history, each
time you talk to a patient.

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:43.129
I swear sometimes like, you
think people are doing work.

00:05:43.129 --> 00:05:45.193
You think they're doing work, but instead,

00:05:45.193 --> 00:05:46.388
here's what they're doing.

00:05:46.388 --> 00:05:48.510
Here's what they're doing. What is that?

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:49.343
That's not work.

00:05:49.343 --> 00:05:50.176
(laughing)

00:05:50.176 --> 00:05:51.639
You're looking at dog pictures.

00:05:51.639 --> 00:05:52.550
(laughing)

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:53.929
Abuse, call HR.

00:05:53.929 --> 00:05:56.470
- Breaking news, they put you in a tutu.

00:05:56.470 --> 00:05:58.610
- That is my tutu. So I
did that for breast cancer

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:00.350
and it is breast cancer awareness month.

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:02.190
So folks, if you're of age, get screened,

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:03.270
talk to your doctor about it.

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:05.240
Today was sort of a short day.

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:06.570
It's only about five, six o'clock.

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:09.063
I thought I'd decide to drive
over to the main hospital

00:06:09.063 --> 00:06:11.561
and show you what we're
doing here in terms of

00:06:11.561 --> 00:06:14.910
preparing for a potential
second wave of COVID.

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:16.490
Yeah, look who run into.

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:18.003
Who is that? Who is this?

00:06:18.003 --> 00:06:20.460
- Hey, get me off of YouTube!

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:21.293
- Okay.

00:06:21.293 --> 00:06:24.127
(dramatic music)

00:06:24.127 --> 00:06:28.000
Now, this ladies and gentlemen,
is a sign I can get behind.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:30.310
The stairs of the hospital.

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:33.140
This is where we got all our exercise in

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:34.130
when we were residents.

00:06:34.130 --> 00:06:36.700
This is where I would sprint
up and down when it was a code

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:38.470
blue, you know, chest
compressions, chest compressions,

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:39.370
chest compressions.

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:40.203
All right.

00:06:40.203 --> 00:06:42.910
We're officially on the
floor that can be used

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:47.150
if there is a need for overflow
of ICU patients specifically

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:49.060
made to battle COVID-19.

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:50.330
A lot of people have been wondering,

00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:53.560
what are hospitals doing to
prepare if there is a second

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:54.530
wave of COVID-19?

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:55.640
Well, this is what we're doing.

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:57.190
There's technological advancements

00:06:57.190 --> 00:06:58.610
that I want to show you
throughout this unit,

00:06:58.610 --> 00:07:01.400
that is a prime example
of collaboration between

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:03.020
the nursing staff, that healthcare staff,

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:05.040
engineering staff, which
you may not think of

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:06.200
as part of healthcare team,

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:08.024
but they're a vital part
of the health care team.

00:07:08.024 --> 00:07:09.680
- Engineering basically made these

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:14.000
eight beds pods for us, and
they gave us a monitor system.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:15.600
So we can look inside all the rooms.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:17.080
So we don't have to go in the room.

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:19.929
And also they gave us these
monitors so that we can adjust

00:07:19.929 --> 00:07:22.630
the physiologic monitors in the room,

00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:23.790
so we don't have to go in.

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:26.560
The other thing they did is during COVID,

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:28.150
when nurses needed something,

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:29.729
they'd have to bang on the door,

00:07:29.729 --> 00:07:33.017
get someone's attention and
scream to the door saying,

00:07:33.017 --> 00:07:34.180
"I need this."

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:36.160
So, intercom system.

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:36.993
- Wow.

00:07:36.993 --> 00:07:38.540
- So all the rooms have intercom systems.

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:41.620
On the med surge units, the doors

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:43.443
had little slits for windows.

00:07:43.443 --> 00:07:44.510
- Yep.

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:46.920
- So here, they made
the windows much bigger,

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:49.970
easy visibility, and we
don't have to peer through

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:50.803
a little slit.

00:07:50.803 --> 00:07:52.738
The other thing is outlets.

00:07:52.738 --> 00:07:54.580
- Oh yeah, something
that we take for granted.

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:55.760
We don't even think about those.

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:57.493
- Outlets on the wall in the hallway.

00:07:57.493 --> 00:07:59.520
- Why should they be in the hallway?

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:01.030
- Because that's where
all the equipment is.

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:01.863
- Right.

00:08:01.863 --> 00:08:03.180
So you don't want to have it
inside the patient's room.

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:05.410
'Cause then if you need to
change it, that's a waste of PPE.

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:06.620
That's a potential exposure.

00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:09.760
- The other great thing
engineering did, listened to us,

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:11.551
is they built these
little holes in the wall.

00:08:11.551 --> 00:08:14.210
What that does is, we feed
our IV tubing through.

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:17.624
Each of the rooms is outfitted
with what we call a scrubber.

00:08:17.624 --> 00:08:20.589
So if you make any room
into negative pressure.

00:08:20.589 --> 00:08:22.070
- Look at that.

00:08:22.070 --> 00:08:23.945
- All the patients had to
be at negative pressure.

00:08:23.945 --> 00:08:25.360
- Exactly, so for those
of you who don't know

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:27.740
what negative pressure
room is, it basically sucks

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:29.800
the air into the room,
keeps the air into the room

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:31.980
so that infection doesn't
potentially seep out

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:33.880
into the environment, getting others sick,

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:36.870
which protects other patients
and nursing staff, doctors,

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:38.130
CMAs, everyone who's around.

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:42.220
So how many total beds are
available on this floor?

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:44.540
- So there's going to be a total of 32.

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:47.040
- 32 beds, and they're
available, ready to use

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:48.140
already if we need them.

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:48.973
- Correct.

00:08:48.973 --> 00:08:49.970
- So we're prepared for the surge.

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:51.400
We're ready to help people out.

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:53.860
And these can be full on ICUs.

00:08:53.860 --> 00:08:55.120
- They can be full on ICUs.

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:56.450
- Wow, okay. So we're ready.

00:08:56.450 --> 00:08:57.900
Is there like a central nursing station?

00:08:57.900 --> 00:08:58.960
- There is.
- Can we see that?

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:01.680
- You have a large monitoring screen here.

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:04.220
The cameras that we can look
at all the rooms right here

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:06.560
and then our central monitoring
station is right here.

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:10.110
- And then you have oxygen
meters, medical air,

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:13.240
the vacuums, everything is
set up, so it could be done

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:16.690
right here without necessarily
entering the patient's room.

00:09:16.690 --> 00:09:17.523
- Correct.

00:09:17.523 --> 00:09:19.220
- Hopefully we won't need
to use this unit much,

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:21.810
but if the situation arises,
we can absolutely know

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:22.900
that we're going to be taken care of.

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:24.239
Our patients are going
to be taken care of,

00:09:24.239 --> 00:09:25.630
our staff is going to be safe.

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:28.250
Do you have any parting words
for YouTube audience here?

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:30.810
- No. Just, you know,
don't feel like, care.

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:32.570
If you're sick, come to the hospital,

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:34.039
we are a safe place to be.

00:09:34.039 --> 00:09:37.340
We have many lessons that we
learned that we've put into

00:09:37.340 --> 00:09:38.480
place already.

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:39.437
And we're here for you.

00:09:39.437 --> 00:09:41.820
- Healthcare hero right here. (laughs)

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:45.480
Alrighty, you guys got the
full tour, the COVID wing.

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:50.340
I am on my way home. Not to
relax, but to do some notes.

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:51.686
Every job has its pros and cons.

00:09:51.686 --> 00:09:53.950
Writing long notes, cons of this job,

00:09:53.950 --> 00:09:56.560
but the pros most definitely outweigh it.

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:59.302
I'd never trade my
career for anything else.

00:09:59.302 --> 00:10:01.885
(gentle music)

